OpenBSD lacks xattrs; store symlink reparse data in regular files with
Wine's '?' suffix, matching ntdll/unix/file.c. Truncate the data when
removing a reparse point so it does not become ordinary file contents.

OpenBSD rounds Wine's computed kevent() timeout up to kernel ticks and adds
an extra tick, so timeout-only wakeups arrive late. Use a periodic EVFILT_TIMER
requested at 1 ms: after its first expiration, kernel rearming omits that
extra tick. Wakeups follow kernel ticks (10 ms at 100 Hz); depending on tick
phase, kevent() may return in under 10 ms, before Wine's requested delay has
elapsed. Wine rechecks deadlines before expiring timers. Keep timer events
outside FD dispatch; fall back to poll if registration fails.
Wine's default 16 ms shared-data refresh wait does not align with 10 ms
ticks; this workaround needs further refinement and testing with user32:msg.

Honor SO_SNDLOWAT for write readiness to avoid EWOULDBLOCK retry loops.

